            <ArticleTitle>Reduction of acute hospital admissions and improvement in outpatient attendance by intensified renal palliative care clinic follow-up: the Hong Kong experience.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>144-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jpainsymman.2014.04.010</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0885-3924(14)00258-9</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">End-stage renal failure patients often fail to attend scheduled renal palliative care clinic (RPCC) follow-up because of acute hospital admissions, causing negative impact on patients' well-being and health care burden.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">The rates of RPCC attendance, emergency department (ED) attendance, and acute hospital admission per patient from January 2013 to June 2013 were analyzed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Patients who had more than one ED visit within three months were invited to intensify their RPCC follow-up schedule for symptom assessment, medical advice, psychosocial-spiritual care, and social worker support in the subsequent three months.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OUTCOMES" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Nineteen patients were included. The rate of ED attendance (2.63 vs. 0.63, P &lt; 0.007) and acute hospital admission (1.59 vs. 0.58, P &lt; 0.009) was reduced significantly after intensified follow-up. Clinic attendance rates improved from 56% to 85%.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS/LESSONS LEARNED" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Our pilot results suggested that intensifying RPCC follow-up minimized the utilization of acute medical services and improved outpatient attendance at RPCC.</AbstractText>
